# =============================================================================
#  Netlify redirect rules
# =============================================================================
#  Everything in frontend/public/ is copied verbatim into dist/ by Vite, so
#  this file ends up at the root of the published site on EVERY build.
#
#  That matters because Netlify reads routing rules from two places:
#    * netlify.toml  -> only when Netlify builds the site from your Git repo
#    * _redirects    -> always, including drag-and-drop ("Netlify Drop") deploys
#
#  Since a Drop deploy only uploads the dist folder, netlify.toml (which lives
#  one level up in frontend/) is never seen. This file is therefore the single
#  source of truth for routing, and it works for both deployment methods.
#
#  Order matters: Netlify applies the FIRST rule that matches.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOTE: there is no /api rule here on purpose.
#
# Netlify's redirect proxy only forwards to standard ports, and the API runs on
# port 8081. A rule here returned 502 and never reached the server. The API is
# handled by netlify/functions/api.js instead, which has no port restriction.
# Change the backend address there (or with the API_ORIGIN environment
# variable in the Netlify UI).
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Single-page-app fallback
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# React Router does the routing in the browser. Without this, opening
# /dashboard directly (or pressing F5 on it) would ask Netlify for a file
# named "dashboard", which does not exist, and return 404.
# Status 200 means "serve index.html for this path" rather than redirecting.
/*  /index.html  200
